## 2. Entry point and configuration

### `ovnctl`

A 25-line shim. Resolves its own real path, inserts that directory at the
front of `sys.path`, imports `ovnsuite.cli.main`, and exits with its return
code.

The explicit `sys.path` manipulation is the point: `sudo` resets the
environment, and the suite is normally invoked by absolute path from cron
or a systemd unit, so relying on the caller's working directory would
break both cases. Nothing needs installing; `pip install .` provides the
same `ovnctl` command on `PATH` if wanted.

### `README.md`

The operator-facing guide. Covers the layout, the command mapping from
shell script to subcommand, the step-selection flags, the global flags,
the output contract, reboot survival, and — most usefully for anyone
maintaining this — a section enumerating the behavioural fixes carried in
the port (see §6).

### `configs/ovn-settings.yaml`

The single source of truth. Sixteen sections:

| section | consumed by |
|---|---|
| `topology` | shared names (`br_int`, `ls_int`, `ls_ext`, `ls_host`, `lr_core`, `host_if`) — identity, not tuning |
| `setup` | external-ids, encap, `system_id`, the three router ports and the host interface (including `host_routes` and `host_route_metric`) |
| `localnet_internal` | the workstation-LAN uplink bridge, transit subnet, gateway priority |
| `localnet_external` | the split subnet (and the legacy second-NIC keys, if still present) |
| `internal_ranges` | "what counts as ours" |
| `external_ranges` | positively-declared external space |
| `engagement` | per-job client subnets, `priority_target` |
| `shadow_ranges` | shadow address space |
| `policy` | the reroute policy tier priorities and `fail_on_unresolved_overlap` |
| `vm_config` | `internal_vms` / `external_vms` — `"name uuid mac ip"` per entry |
| `vm_isolation` | `isolated_vms` — `"name uuid ip"` (no MAC) |
| `acls` | port group definitions, port sets, declarative rules |
| `acl_log` | where `acl_log` records land |
| `vm_names` | display-name mapping |
| `pcap` | capture directory, targets, rotation, snaplen, mirror prefix |
| `diagnose` | check tuning |

`[setup].host_route_metric` is the one key here whose value has to agree
with something outside the file. Both owners of the host routes —
the NetworkManager keyfile and the `ip route replace` in `setup` and
`reconcile` — install the same prefixes, and `replace` matches on
*(destination, metric)*. Left unset, NetworkManager picks its own default
for an OVS internal port and the kernel gives an unqualified `ip route`
metric 0, so neither owner can see or replace the other's copy and every
prefix ends up installed twice. Any value works as long as it is set;
`--install-nm-profile` warns when it is not.

The file header documents the format constraints it must stay inside: two
levels only, `key: value` at 2-space indent, lists as `key:` followed by
4-space `- "item"` entries, comments after unquoted values only, no
anchors, no deeper nesting, no multi-line values.

### `configs/ovn-internal-ranges.conf`

A legacy bash array — `OUR_INTERNAL_RANGES=( ... )` — retained as a
fallback source for the internal range list when `[internal_ranges]` is
absent.

Its long comment header is the load-bearing part: the external-to-us
network reachable through the ASA also uses RFC1918 addressing, so
"is it RFC1918?" is not a valid test for "is it ours?". A blanket
`10/8 + 172.16/12 + 192.168/16` exclusion would either misroute partner
traffic through the internal path, or block the isolated VMs' only
legitimate path. Hence an explicit list.

The suite **parses** this file rather than sourcing it, so a stray command
in a config file cannot execute.

### `state/ovn-deploy-state`

Generated, not authored. One flag per line:

```
key <ISO-8601 timestamp> boot=<kernel boot id>
```

Written by each command on completion, removed by `--remove`, truncated by
`ovnctl delete`. See `state.py`.

