## 3. Core library — `ovnsuite/`

### `__init__.py`

Package docstring describing the expected on-disk layout and the
subcommand/step model. Defines `__version__ = "2.0.0"`, exported through
`ovnctl --version`.

### `paths.py` (85 lines)

Layout resolution. Ports `_ovn_pick` / `_ovn_find_settings` /
`_ovn_find_state` from the shell suite.

| function | returns |
|---|---|
| `suite_root()` | parent of the package directory, or `$OVN_ROOT` |
| `conf_dir()` | `<root>/configs` if it is a directory, else `<root>` |
| `state_dir()` | `<root>/state` if either `state/` or `configs/` exists, else `<root>` |
| `settings_file()` | `<conf_dir>/ovn-settings.yaml` |
| `ranges_file()` | `<conf_dir>/ovn-internal-ranges.conf` |
| `state_file()` | `<state_dir>/ovn-deploy-state` |

Environment overrides, each taking precedence over the derived value:
`OVN_CONFIG_FILE`, `OVN_CONF_DIR`, `OVN_STATE_DIR`, `OVN_STATE_FILE`,
`OVN_ROOT`, `INTERNAL_RANGES_CONF`.

The `state_dir()` logic deliberately checks for a *sibling* `configs/`:
that proves the split layout is in use, so `state/` is the right home even
if it has not been created yet.

### `config.py` (355 lines)

Loader for `ovn-settings.yaml` — a faithful port of the awk parser from
`ovn-config.sh`, **not** a general YAML reader.

That is a deliberate decision, documented in the module docstring. The
real settings file contains constructs a strict parser and a full YAML
parser disagree on: bare values with a trailing `#`, `key: []` acting as a
list header that later `- item` lines still append to, duplicated keys
inside commented-out examples. Matching the original parser exactly means
a config that worked under the shell suite works here unchanged.

**Parsing.** Three regexes drive it — a section line, a list item, a key
line. Every value lands under a `(section, key)` tuple holding either a
`str` or a `list[str]`. `_strip_value()` ports the awk `strip()`: a quoted
value ends at its *closing* quote and the rest of the line is discarded,
without which a trailing comment containing a quote is swallowed into the
value.

**Accessors.**

| method | semantics |
|---|---|
| `cfg(section, key)` | **required** — raises `ConfigError` naming the key |
| `cfg_opt(section, key, default)` | genuinely optional |
| `cfg_int` / `cfg_int_opt` / `cfg_bool` | typed variants |
| `cfg_array(section, key)` | list, or `None` if absent/empty (lets callers keep their own defaults) |
| `cfg_list(section, key)` | same, but always a list |
| `require(*"section:key")` | validates every required setting **before** anything is changed, reporting all misses at once |

There are no built-in defaults anywhere. A missing key stops the run with
a message naming it, rather than silently deploying something that does
not match the yaml the operator is reading.

**In-place editors.** `scalar_set`, `list_add`, `list_del` let a command
update the settings file at runtime (e.g. `localnet-external --add-target`
adding an engagement subnet) without hand-editing. They write via a
temp-file rename and operate on the same strict subset the parser
understands. `list_add` rewrites `key: []` to `key:` so the appended item
parses.

**`load_legacy_ranges(path)`.** Regex-extracts `OUR_INTERNAL_RANGES=( ... )`
from the legacy conf file — parsing, not sourcing.

### `context.py` (378 lines)

The port of `ovn-common.sh` and `ovn-dryrun.sh`. Everything shared by
every subcommand.

**The output contract**, unchanged from the shell suite:

| condition | output |
|---|---|
| success | one summary line, nothing else |
| `-v` | full step-by-step narration |
| `--dry-run` | the commands that would run, and nothing else |
| warnings | always, on stderr |
| errors | always, on stderr, then exit non-zero |

**`Ctx`** — the per-run dataclass holding flags (`verbose`, `dry_run`,
`listing`), counters (`changes`, `warnings`), the query timeout, and the
lazily-loaded `Config`. It exposes:

- *Config pass-throughs* — `cfg`, `cfg_opt`, `cfg_int`, `cfg_bool`,
  `cfg_array`, `cfg_list`, `require_cfg` — so command modules read the way
  the shell did.
- *Output* — `log` (narration, `-v` only), `say` (must-see even on a quiet
  run), `out` (tables), `warn`, `err`, `die`, `finish` (the single `OK:`
  line), `dr_head` / `dr_banner` (dry-run section headings).
- *Execution* — `run()` for **mutating** commands (printed under
  `--dry-run`, executed otherwise, increments `changes`) and `q()` /
  `qout()` for **read-only queries**.
- *Preconditions* — `require_root`, `require_cmd`, `have`.
- *systemd* — `unit_exists`, `unit_active`.

Two details in here carry real weight:

- **Queries are never wrapped by dry-run.** The printed command list has
  to reflect what would actually change *given the current state of the
  system*, which means the queries that decide that must really run.
- **Queries are always bounded** (15s default). `ovn-nbctl` and
  `ovn-sbctl` wait on the db forever by default, and the commands that
  query them are exactly the ones run while that db is unreachable. A
  timeout yields a normal failed `Result` with rc 124 (as `timeout(1)`
  uses), so every existing `if not res.ok` path already handles it.

`listing=True` (set for `--list-steps`) skips the root and command
preconditions: the step listing is documentation, and documentation you
can only read on a healthy deployment is not much use when the deployment
is broken.

**`Abort`** — fatal error with a pre-formatted message and an exit code;
`cli.main` turns it into the process status.

**`Result`** — `returncode` / `stdout` / `stderr`, with `.ok`, `.lines`,
and `__bool__` so `if ctx.run(...)` reads naturally.

**`Colour`** — ANSI codes, but only when stdout is a TTY and `NO_COLOR` is
unset. `--no-color` exports `NO_COLOR`, so honouring the variable covers
the flag, and a redirected run stays plain text without anyone
remembering.

**`trace_verdict(output)`** — the single most consequential function in
the file. Turns `ovn-trace` output into `ALLOW` / `DROP` / `UNKNOWN`.

northd compiles an ACL drop into several different shapes depending on
options unrelated to the verdict: bare `drop;` on a stateless pipeline,
`ct_commit { ct_mark.blocked = 1; }` on the established branch of a
stateful one, and — once the ACL carries a name or logging — the log
action with the drop left implicit *as a C comment*. Matching only
`drop;` reports the last two as ALLOW. Naming the ACLs is what made every
isolation check report "not blocked" on a deployment that was enforcing
correctly.

`UNKNOWN` exists for the same class of reason: this used to return ALLOW
for any unrecognised output, including empty output, so a trace that
timed out or failed to parse was indistinguishable from a permitted
packet. ALLOW now requires positive evidence — real output always contains
an `ingress(dp=...` line.

`shquote()` / `format_cmd()` render argv lists shell-safely but readably
for the dry-run listing.

### `state.py` (196 lines)

The deployment tracker (port of `ovn-state.sh`).

Six keys, in documented run order: `setup`, `localnet-internal`,
`localnet-external`, `vm-config`, `vm-isolation`, `acls`.

**The tracker is advisory.** Commands never hard-fail because a flag is
missing — the OVN database itself is always the source of truth. The
tracker records what was *intended*, so `diagnose` can tell "not
configured yet" apart from "broken".

**`Tracker`** — `mark`, `unmark`, `has`, `clear`, `exists`, `show`,
`flags`, plus the boot-awareness methods below. `exists()` distinguishes
"tracking is in use and nothing is deployed" from "tracking has never been
used".

**Boot ids.** Each entry records `/proc/sys/kernel/random/boot_id`. The
tracker records intent and lives on disk, so it survives a reboot
unchanged — which meant a post-reboot `diagnose` read `setup=True`, gated
away the checks that would have caught the problem, and reported a
deployment no longer realised on the running system. `from_earlier_boot()`
and `any_from_earlier_boot()` tell "deployed and live" apart from
"deployed during an earlier boot, runtime half may be gone". An unknown id
on either side returns False — absence of evidence must not manufacture a
warning.

**Dry-run safety.** Writes are no-ops when `ctx.dry_run` is set. The shell
suite called `state_mark` unconditionally, so `./ovn-setup.sh --dry-run`
left a `setup` flag behind and every later diagnose believed the
deployment existed.

**`record(ctx, key, runner)`** — marks a component deployed *only if the
whole command ran*. Recording `setup` after `--only switches` would tell
diagnose to expect a router, gateway ports and a host interface that were
never created, and report each as a failure rather than "not deployed
yet". A partial run leaves the flag alone and logs why.

### `steps.py` (128 lines)

The step framework — the part the shell suite could not do.

`Step` is a `(name, desc, fn)` dataclass. `StepRunner` collects them via
`add()` and exposes:

- `list_steps()` — numbered listing with descriptions
- `select(only, skip, start)` — resolves the selection flags
- `run(args)` — executes, returning `False` if it only listed

Steps always execute in **declaration order** regardless of the order they
are named on the command line. Selection changes *which* steps run, never
the sequence, because the sequence is the part that has to hold.

`runner.partial` is set when a subset ran; `state.record()` reads it to
decide whether claiming the component as deployed would be honest.

`add_step_args(parser)` attaches `--list-steps`, `--only A,B`, `--skip A,B`
and `--from A` to a subcommand parser.

An unknown step name raises `Abort` listing the known ones.

### `netcalc.py` (113 lines)

IPv4 CIDR arithmetic (port of `ovn-netcalc.sh`), implemented on stdlib
`ipaddress`.

It exists for one reason: the deployment declares both "our internal
ranges" and "external-via-ASA ranges", and both may use RFC1918. If those
lists intersect, a destination-based routing policy becomes ambiguous —
one prefix cannot have two next-hops. Silently absorbing that ambiguity
presents as "that host is mysteriously unreachable", so every command that
builds policies or ACLs calls in here first and says so out loud.

| function | purpose |
|---|---|
| `cidr_valid` / `cidr_bounds` | parse and validate (bare IP → /32; host bits masked off, not rejected) |
| `cidr_overlap` / `cidr_contains` | the two containment tests |
| `cidr_describe_overlap` | the intersection as `first - last`, for the error message |
| `find_overlaps(a, b)` | every colliding pair across two lists |
| `validate_ranges` | the entries that are not valid CIDRs |
| `ip_in_ranges` | membership test |
| `set_literal` | build an OVN address-set literal `{a,b,c}` |

### `ovn.py` (580 lines)

Thin helpers around `ovs-vsctl`, `ovn-nbctl`, `ovn-sbctl` and `ovn-trace`.

Almost all read-only. Mutating commands stay as explicit
`ctx.run("ovn-nbctl", ...)` calls in the command modules so they remain
visible in the `--dry-run` listing exactly as the shell version printed
them.

**ovs-vsctl** — `br_exists`, `list_br`, `list_ports`, `iface_type`,
`external_id`, `has_external_id`, `iface_for_iface_id`, `iface_id_map`
(one query for the whole Interface table), `iface_id_of`, `mirror_exists`,
`iface_health`, `iface_is_healthy`.

**`iface_health(ctx, iface)`** returns `(ofport, error)` and is the test
for "does this interface actually work", as distinct from "does a row
describing it exist". ovs-vswitchd fills `ofport` and clears `error` only
once it has really opened the netdev; until then the row can look entirely
correct — right name, right type, right `iface-id`, sitting on br-int —
while the datapath has nothing behind it and ovn-controller has no port to
bind. `admin_state` and `link_state` read as the *empty set* in that
state rather than `down`, which is easy to skim past. `ofport -1` is
unambiguous. `(0, "")` means the interface does not exist at all, so
callers can tell "no row" from "row with no datapath".

**ovn-nbctl** — existence probes (`lr_exists`, `ls_exists`, `lsp_exists`,
`lrp_present`), port-group access (`pg_uuid`, `pg_exists`, `pg_members`),
`policy_uuids`, `policies_with_match`, `port_security`, `acl_list`.

**`pg_snapshot(ctx)`** — every port group with members and ACLs resolved,
in four queries regardless of group count rather than three per group.
`ports` and `acls` are fetched separately on purpose: asking for both
reference columns at once returned unreliable results for one of them, and
a silently short member list reads as drift that isn't there.
`Port_Group.ports` holds row uuids, translated back to logical port names
here because that is what the inventory and the yaml speak.

**`acl_index(ctx)`** — keyed on `(direction, priority, match)`, which is
what `ovn-nbctl --may-exist` treats as an ACL's identity, so it answers
"have I already created this one, and does it carry the name, logging and
meter I meant it to?".

**`add_acl(...)`** — the one mutating helper here, because it carries a
version-compatibility fallback two command modules would otherwise each
copy. `ovn-nbctl` grew `--name` for `acl-add` alongside the ACL table's
name column; support is probed once per process by *trying* it, since a
version check would have to map release numbers to behaviour and the
command itself is authoritative. A failure is only treated as a version
problem if the complaint names the option — any other failure surfaces as
it always did rather than being silently retried into a differently-broken
state.

**ovn-sbctl** — `chassis_names`, `first_chassis`, `chassis_uuid_names`,
`port_binding_chassis`, `port_binding_chassis_name` (resolves the *name*,
not merely "is one set"), `port_binding_type`, `port_binding_ports`.

**OVSDB JSON helpers** — `_json_rows`, `_json_map`, `_scalar` (an unset
optional column arrives as `['set', []]`; a bare `str()` of that yields
the literal text), `uuid_list`, `ref_is_set`, `nb_json`, `sb_json`,
`sb_records`.

**`sync_sb(ctx)`** — blocks until northd has compiled NB into SB.
`ovn-nbctl` returns as soon as the Northbound db is written; northd
compiles asynchronously; `ovn-trace` reads the *Southbound* db. A trace
run immediately after creating an ACL can therefore legitimately report
the behaviour from before it existed. That is a race, not a flaky test,
and on a busy or freshly-purged db the window is comfortably wide.
`--wait=sb sync` closes it.

**`trace(ctx, datapath, expr)`** — runs `ovn-trace` with a 60s timeout
(`TRACE_TIMEOUT`) and returns combined stdout+stderr. The default 15s
query ceiling timed traces out routinely on large deployments, and a
timed-out trace produced empty output every caller then read as ALLOW.
Pair with `context.trace_verdict`.

### `inventory.py` (142 lines)

The VM inventory, shared by `vm-config`, `vm-attach`, `vm-isolation`,
`acl`, `pcap` and `diagnose`.

Reads `[vm_config].internal_vms` / `.external_vms` (`"name uuid mac ip"`)
and `[vm_isolation].isolated_vms` (`"name uuid ip"`, no MAC).

`VM` is a frozen dataclass: `name`, `uuid`, `mac`, `ip`, `group`
(`internal`/`external`), `switch`, plus a `mac_lc` property.

`Inventory` exposes `all`, `internal`, `external`, `isolated`,
`non_isolated`, and the lookups `by_name`, `by_ip`, `by_uuid`, `by_mac`.

**`select(selector)`** resolves the selector syntax used wherever a group
of VMs is named:

```
all | internal | external | isolated | all-except-isolated
names=vm1,vm2
ips=1.2.3.4,5.6.7.8
```

An unknown selector or an unresolvable name/IP raises `Abort` rather than
returning an empty list — silently returning nothing is how a port group
ends up with no members and an ACL ends up applying to nothing.

### `libvirtutil.py` (76 lines)

virsh helpers. **libvirt is optional throughout the suite.**

Taps are resolved from OVS by `iface-id` wherever possible, because
ovn-controller binds a port by matching `external-ids:iface-id` on a local
interface — OVS holds the definitive mapping, and it works on a host with
no libvirt CLI at all. virsh is used to put a human-readable domain name
on things, and as a fallback for a tap with no `iface-id` set.

`DomIface(domain, tap, mac)`; `available`, `running_domains`,
`domiflist` (parses `virsh domiflist`, skipping the two header lines),
`interfaces` (every interface of every running domain in one pass),
`mac_maps` (mac→tap and mac→domain).

### `identity.py` (333 lines)

Chassis identity — the one piece of state that must not drift.

An OVN chassis is identified by `external-ids:system-id` in the local OVS
db. Everything downstream is keyed off that name: the `Chassis` row in the
SB db, every `Port_Binding.chassis` reference, every `Gateway_Chassis` pin
in the NB db. Change the name and all of it silently stops matching — the
bindings and pins still exist, they just point at a chassis that no longer
runs anything.

Two things make the name drift across a reboot, and the suite used to walk
into both:

1. `ovs-ctl start --system-id=random` (how `openvswitch.service` is
   normally invoked) reads `/etc/openvswitch/system-id.conf`, generating a
   random UUID if the file is absent, and writes it into
   `external-ids:system-id` at **every** boot. Whatever setup put there is
   overwritten.
2. Falling back to `hostname` when no id is configured — hostname vs FQDN
   vs a DHCP-supplied name is exactly the kind of thing that changes
   underneath a machine.

So the canonical name comes from `[setup].system_id`, is written to **both**
`external-ids` and `system-id.conf`, and is re-asserted on every run.

| function | purpose |
|---|---|
| `configured_opt` / `configured` | the canonical name; the latter aborts with instructions if unset |
| `local` | `external-ids:system-id` as it stands right now |
| `conf_file_value` | the id ovs-ctl will re-assert at the next boot |
| `registered` / `is_registered` | chassis names in the SB db |
| `stale` | registered chassis that are not the canonical one |
| `write_system_id_conf` | persist where ovs-ctl looks at boot |
| `assert_external_ids` | reconcile all four keys, individually |
| `restart_controller` | via systemd, falling back to `ovn-ctl` |
| `wait_for_chassis` | block until the name appears in the SB db |
| `purge_stale_chassis` | **destructive, opt-in** — remove non-canonical rows |
| `pin_target` | the chassis a gateway port should be pinned to |
| `repin_gateway` | pin, removing any pin to another chassis |
| `gateway_pins` / `stale_pins` | audit the `Gateway_Chassis` table |

Notes worth carrying:

- `assert_external_ids` is deliberately **not** gated on "is ovn-remote
  already set". conf.db is persistent, so after the first deploy that gate
  is always true and the other three keys — system-id above all — are
  never looked at again. Each key is compared and set individually.
- `restart_controller` exists because ovn-controller reads
  `external-ids:system-id` once, at startup. Changing it under a running
  controller leaves the process registered as the old name, which looks
  fine to every check that only asks whether *a* chassis exists.
- `pin_target` aborts if the configured name is not registered. A pin to
  an unregistered chassis is a silent black hole: northd accepts it, the
  `cr-` port never binds, nothing reaches the physical network.
- `repin_gateway` deletes old pins explicitly because
  `lrp-set-gateway-chassis` **adds** a pin rather than replacing one — a
  drifted host otherwise ends up with both.
- `purge_stale_chassis` also sweeps `Chassis_Private` by uuid, since
  `chassis-del` leaves it behind on some versions and a name containing
  characters the CLI won't take never matched at all.

### `cli.py` (171 lines)

The single entry point. Builds the argparse root, registers every
subcommand by importing `commands/<name>.py` and calling its `register()`,
constructs the `Ctx`, and dispatches.

**Global flags**: `-v/--verbose`, `-n/--dry-run`, `--config PATH`,
`--state-file PATH`, `--no-color`, `--version`.

**`_split_globals(argv)`** hoists post-subcommand global flags to the
front. argparse puts global options on the root parser only, so
`ovnctl setup -n` would otherwise be an error — and everyone types it that
way. Anything after a bare `--` is left alone.

**`_apply_path_overrides`** — `--config` and `--state-file` are friendlier
spellings of the environment variables the path resolver already honours,
so they are simply exported.

**`run_argv(argv)`** is public so `deploy` and `runbook` can re-enter the
CLI in-process rather than re-executing the interpreter.

**Exit codes**, produced by `main()`:

| code | meaning |
|---|---|
| 0 | success |
| 1 | failure, including `diagnose` finding problems |
| 2 | usage error (no subcommand) |
| 130 | interrupted |

`BrokenPipeError` is caught and returns 0 — `ovnctl show | head` is not an
error worth a traceback.

---

## 4. Command modules — `ovnsuite/commands/`

Every module follows the same shape: `NAME`, `HELP`, an optional
`REQUIRED_CFG` tuple, a `register(subparsers)` function, a class holding
the work as one method per step, and a `main(ctx, args)` that validates
config, checks preconditions, builds a `StepRunner`, runs it, and records
state.

### `setup.py` (565 lines) — `ovnctl setup`

Port of `ovn-setup.sh`. Base topology for a single-node OVN environment.

Steps:

1. `external-ids` — configure OVS `external-ids` (via `identity.assert_external_ids`)
2. `ovn-controller` — start the control plane, wait for br-int
3. `switches` — create `ls-int-vm`, `ls-ext-vm`, `ls-host`
4. `logical-router` — create `lr-core` and its gateway ports
5. `attach-switches` — attach each switch to the router
6. `host-interface` — create `host-if`, address it, add routes

`REQUIRED_CFG` covers 21 keys across `[setup]` and `[topology]`.
`system_id` is required with no fallback — there used to be a
`socket.gethostname()` default here, and a hostname that changes between
boots renames the chassis, after which every `Port_Binding` and gateway pin
references an identity nothing is running under.

The class also carries `_br_int_diagnosis` (explains *why* br-int never
appeared, rather than just timing out), `_kernel_mac` / `_fix_kernel_mac`,
`_fix_addresses`, `_add_routes` and `_verify_and_report`.
`self.identity_changed` is set when `external_ids()` had to rewrite
system-id, so later steps and `reconcile` know the chassis was just
re-registered under a different name.

**`_ensure_ovs_port()`** owns the creation of the internal port, and is
the reason `host-interface` can repair as well as build. The guard used to
be "if `port-to-br` fails, add-port; otherwise skip", which conflates the
existence of a port row with a working interface. Once a broken row
existed nothing would ever rebuild it: `setup` and `reconcile` both went
on re-asserting the `iface-id` on a dead row for as long as anyone cared
to re-run them, while `diagnose` reported an unbound port and pointed at
`reconcile`. It now checks `ovn.iface_health()`, and deletes and recreates
the port when `ofport` is not positive, reporting what ovs-vswitchd said.

**`_link_kind()` / `_reclaim_name()` / `PLACEHOLDER_KINDS`.** Kernel
device names are a single global namespace: whoever creates a device
called `host-if` first owns it, and ovs-vswitchd then cannot create its
internal port, failing with

```
could not add network device host-if to ofproto (File exists)
```

The most common way to get there is a NetworkManager profile of a
device-creating type (`dummy`, `bridge`, `ovs-interface`) bound to that
interface name — and because such a profile recreates the device at every
boot, this needs handling rather than reporting once. `_link_kind()` reads
the device kind from `ip -d link show`; `_reclaim_name()` deletes the
squatter when it is a `dummy` or `veth` (placeholders, by definition not
carrying anything) and aborts with instructions for any other kind, since
a bridge or a bond might be. It runs only after the OVS port row has been
deleted, so it can never fire against a device OVS is legitimately
driving, and it only ever looks at the configured `host_if` name.

**`_add_routes()` / `_route_metrics()`** install the host routes at
`[setup].host_route_metric`. `ip route replace` matches on *(destination,
metric)*, so a copy of a prefix at any other metric is invisible to it and
survives alongside the one being written. With NetworkManager holding the
same routes at its own metric and this code writing them at metric 0, the
two owners could not see each other's copies and every prefix ended up
installed twice, one extra pair per reconcile. `_route_metrics()` reports
every metric a prefix currently sits at so the strays can be removed
before the canonical route is written.

### `localnet_internal.py` (337 lines) — `ovnctl localnet-internal`

Port of `ovn-localnet-internal.sh`. Gives the VMs behind `lr-core` a path
to the rest of the internal infrastructure via a localnet port bridging OVN
out to a physical NIC.

**This is not an internet gateway** — no NAT is configured, traffic leaves
and returns with real `172.31.x.x` addresses.

Steps:

1. `check-router` — verify `lr-core` exists
2. `bridge-nic` — create `br-internal` and enslave the NIC
3. `bridge-mapping` — extend `ovn-bridge-mappings`
4. `transit-switch` — transit switch + localnet port
5. `attach-router` — router port + gateway chassis pin
6. `routes` — the `0.0.0.0/0` route to the first-hop firewall
7. `confirm-no-nat` — warn if NAT is configured on `lr-core`

`--remove` tears down just this gateway. The `phys_nic` setting must be a
*spare* NIC cabled into the workstation LAN segment — pointing it at the
management NIC costs you the SSH session, so confirm from console/IPMI
first.

### `localnet_external.py` (705 lines) — `ovnctl localnet-external`

Port of `ovn-localnet-external.sh`. Logical router **policy** that forces
traffic to the ASA even when the ordinary route table would have kept it
inside OVN.

**What changed from the shell version.** The shell script still built a
second physical uplink (`br-asa` on its own NIC, `ls-ext-asa`, `lrp-asa`)
and called `require_cfg` for keys that no longer exist in the settings file
— `[localnet_external].br_asa`, `.phys_nic`, `.ln_asa`, `.lrp_asa`,
`.ls_ext_asa`, `.physnet_label`, `.policy_priority`,
`.gateway_chassis_priority` and the whole `[asa_addressing]` section. The
yaml documents that section as policy-only since the second NIC was
removed. As written, the script aborted on a clean checkout.

So: the policy tiers are the primary path and always run. The legacy
physical-uplink steps still exist and run **only** if those keys are
actually present, so a deployment that still has the second NIC keeps
working unchanged.

Priority tiers, higher evaluated first:

| priority | tier | meaning |
|---|---|---|
| 300 | source-override | listed VMs go to the ASA regardless of destination |
| 250 | engagement | per-job client subnets |
| 200 | shadow | anything addressed into shadow space → ASA |
| 150 | external | positively-declared external ranges → ASA |
| 100 | split | "src subnet AND not internal" |

Steps: `check-router`, `validate-ranges`, `bridge-nic` (legacy),
`bridge-mapping` (legacy), `transit-switch` (legacy), `attach-router`
(legacy), `policy`.

Additional modes: `--trace SRC DST` (prove a tier does what you think),
`--remove`, and the engagement-target editors `--list-targets`,
`--add-target CIDR`, `--del-target CIDR`, `--clear-targets`, which write
back to the settings file via `config.list_add` / `list_del` and then
reapply the policy.

`_validate_next_hop()` implements a rule the yaml documented but nothing
ever checked: `next_hop` must sit inside `transit_subnet`. It is now an
error when set explicitly and off-link, and a warning when inherited from
`lan_gateway`.

### `vm_config.py` (161 lines) — `ovnctl vm-config`

Port of `ovn-vm-config.sh`. Creates the OVN logical switch ports for the
VMs in `[vm_config]`.

UUIDs and MACs come from the settings file and are pinned to the libvirt
domain XMLs — keep the two in sync. The logical switch port is **named
after the VM's UUID**, which is also what belongs in
`<virtualport><parameters interfaceid='...'/>`, so libvirt, OVS and OVN all
agree on one port identity. Every port is tagged with
`external-ids:vm-name` for later correlation.

Steps: `check-switches`, `internal-ports`, `external-ports`. `--remove`
removes these ports only.

Under `-v`, `print_summary()` emits the inventory table plus the exact
libvirt interface XML block to paste into each domain.

### `vm_isolation.py` (628 lines) — `ovnctl vm-isolation`

Port of `ovn-vm-isolation.sh`. Fully isolates the listed VMs from all
internal infrastructure — no other internal host, VM, or the gateway itself
can reach them, and they cannot reach anything internal. Their only path
anywhere is out through `lr-core`'s reroute policy to the ASA; console
access stays available via VNC/KVM regardless of network state.

Mechanism: an OVN port group with two drop ACLs (`from-lport` and
`to-lport`) matching the ranges in `[internal_ranges]` — **not** all of
RFC1918, because the external-to-us network these VMs are allowed to reach
also uses RFC1918 addressing.

Two properties worth knowing:

- Same-subnet (L2) traffic is caught too, since `from-lport`/`to-lport`
  ACLs apply before routing decisions — lateral movement to a non-member
  on the same broadcast domain is blocked exactly like anything else.
- ARP is not touched (the match is scoped to `ip4`), so L2 resolution of
  the gateway's MAC still works; it just never results in permitted IP
  traffic.

Steps: `check-ports`, `port-group`, `acls`, `verify-scope`.

`verify_scope()` proves non-members are unaffected — both statically
(matches are scoped with `inport`/`outport == @group`) and live, by
tracing a real non-member. `_explain_unblocked()` renders a readable
explanation when a trace comes back ALLOW where DROP was expected.
`--trace SRC DST` and `--remove` are the other modes.

### `vm_attach.py` (252 lines) — `ovnctl vm-attach`

Port of `ovn-vm-attach.sh`. Re-attaches running VMs' tap interfaces to
br-int with the correct `iface-id`, without restarting the guests.

Why it exists: deleting br-int (as `ovnctl delete` does) detaches every
running VM's tap from OVS. libvirt only plugs a tap in when a domain
*starts*, so after a teardown + redeploy the guests keep running with a tap
attached to nothing — their ports show `DOWN (no chassis)` forever.
Rebooting works only because it restarts the guests.

Taps are matched to logical ports **by MAC** using `[vm_config]`, so a
mismatch is reported rather than silently wired to the wrong port.

Steps: `check-bridge`, `attach`, `wait`, `status`.

`DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 60` (`--timeout` overrides). This used to be a flat
3-second sleep followed by an immediate status print, which is why
`ovnctl deploy` could report every VM as `DOWN (no chassis)` on a
deployment that was fine thirty seconds later. Claiming is not instant, and
it is slowest in exactly the case where this command matters most: right
after `delete --purge-db`, when ovn-controller is rebuilding its whole view.

`--status` shows what is attached vs not and changes nothing.

### `acl.py` (727 lines) — `ovnctl acl`

Port of `ovn-acl.sh`. Applies the declarative rules in `[acls]`.

Port groups are built from the VM inventory (so membership can never drift
from `[vm_config]`), and every rule is scoped to its group with
`inport`/`outport == @group` — without that scoping an ACL applies to
every port on the switch.

Rule format:

```
<name> <direction> <priority> <action> <group> <spec>...

direction  to-lport    evaluated on the way IN to a group member
           from-lport  evaluated on the way OUT of a group member
action     allow | allow-related | drop | reject
spec       src=CIDR[,CIDR]   dst=CIDR[,CIDR]   src=any
           src=internal / dst=internal   expands to [internal_ranges]
           tcp=PORT | tcp=@portset       udp=PORT | udp=@portset      icmp
           log             log every packet this rule matches
           log=SEVERITY    the same, at alert|warning|notice|info|debug
           meter=NAME      rate-limit this rule's logging
```

Higher priority wins. Anything with no matching rule is **allowed** (OVN's
default), so these rules only constrain what they name.

Logging is per rule and off by default — on a busy segment an allow rule
that logs is a firehose. `log_sink()` writes an rsyslog drop-in that files
`acl_log` records into `[acl_log].directory`; the `& stop` in the template
matters, because without it every ACL record is *also* appended to
`/var/log/messages`, which is how a chatty allow rule takes out the system
log rather than just its own file.

Steps: `port-groups`, `log-meter`, `rules`, `log-sink`.

Modes (mutually exclusive): `--remove`, `--verify` (ovn-trace the key
allow/deny cases), `--list`, `--audit`.

Two ordering decisions in `main()`: `--audit` runs *before* the
`acls.enabled` check, because "we turned ACLs off — is the policy actually
gone?" is exactly what an audit should answer; and `--list-steps` is
answered even when the feature is switched off, or "what would this do?"
becomes unanswerable precisely when you are deciding whether to switch it
on.

`name_existing_rules()` back-fills names onto ACLs created before naming
was supported — relevant because an unnamed logging ACL produces log
records nobody can trace back to a rule.

### `acl_audit.py` (567 lines) — `ovnctl acl --audit`

The conformance engine. Every rule in `[acls].rules` is a test case.

The expected match string is rebuilt with `ACLManager.build_match` — the
same code that deploys it — so the audit cannot pass a rule the deployer
would write differently. A separate reimplementation here would agree with
the yaml and disagree with reality, which is the one failure mode an audit
must not have.

Three questions, in order:

1. **Is the configuration sound?** Direction, priority range (0–32767),
   action, port-set and port-group references, CIDR syntax, priority
   collisions. No db access — a rule that cannot be right is worth catching
   before asking whether it is deployed.
2. **Is every configured rule in the NB db**, with the same direction,
   priority, action and match?
3. **Does the db hold anything the configuration does not account for?**
   An ACL nobody declared is policy nobody reviewed. This matters more than
   it looks: the deployer uses `acl-add --may-exist`, so *editing* a rule's
   match adds a second ACL and leaves the original in force — and the old
   one is invisible to every other command in the suite.

Output contract: a pass is one line. A failure prints what was expected,
what is actually there, and the command that fixes it, because whoever
reads the failure is usually not whoever wrote the rule. `_norm()` makes
the comparison whitespace-insensitive and `_first_diff()` reports the
column where two matches diverge — a spacing difference is worth reporting,
but not as the same failure as a genuinely different rule.

Exit status is 1 if any check failed, so this can gate a deployment.

### `pcap.py` (920 lines) — `ovnctl pcap`

Port of `ovn-pcap.sh`. Captures traffic from VM logical ports using OVS
port mirroring (SPAN).

Per target VM:

1. a dedicated internal capture port is created on br-int
   (`<mirror_prefix>-<vm>`) — its name never changes, unlike the VM's tap
2. an OVS mirror copies the tap's ingress **and** egress into it
3. tcpdump writes rotated pcaps

The capture survives a VM restart: only the mirror's source port needs
re-pointing (`--restart`), while the tcpdump process and its output files
stay put.

**Layout.** Two directories under the one configured path:

```
<directory>/inflight/[<vm>/]     tcpdump's open, still-growing file
<directory>/completed/[<vm>/]    closed files, nothing else
```

On rotation the hook moves the closed file across. Both sit on the same
filesystem, so that move is a plain rename — atomic, with no window in
which a partial file is visible under its final name. Whatever collects the
captures takes everything in `completed/` and never has to work out which
file tcpdump still has open. `_check_same_filesystem()` enforces the
precondition.

No compression, and no `chown` anywhere. Access is granted by POSIX ACL to
the account tcpdump drops to (`-Z`); ownership and mode are left exactly as
provisioning left them (`resolve_drop_user`, `grant_capture_dir`,
`grant_rundir`).

Modes rather than steps — `--start`, `--stop`, `--restart`, `--status`
(default). `--list-steps` prints the mode list instead. PID files live
under `/run/ovn-pcap`. `check_disk()` enforces `[pcap].min_free_mb` and
`max_files`; `sweep_inflight()` reconciles leftovers from an unclean stop.

`--start` with `[pcap].enabled: false` logs why nothing started and
**exits 0**. It used to abort. A config switch set to off is a decision
someone made, and a correctly-refused no-op is not a failure of anything —
but it meant every `deploy` on a host that has simply chosen not to
capture printed an `ERROR`, a `WARN … exited 1`, and a skipped stage in
the summary, on every single run. Permanent expected noise is how people
learn to stop reading output. Leaving `enabled` false still guarantees a
capture cannot begin without being recorded in config, which is the point
of the switch.

### `diagnose.py` (1290 lines) — `ovnctl diagnose`

Port of `ovn-diagnose.sh`. Walks the OVN/OVS stack layer by layer to find
where connectivity is breaking. The largest module in the suite.

**Tracker-aware.** Checks are gated on what has actually been deployed, so
"gateway port missing" is only a FAIL if a localnet command recorded itself
as run, and a clean SKIP otherwise. Conversely, ambiguous results become
definitive: tracker says `localnet-internal` ran **and** no chassisredirect
port exists = hard FAIL, no hedging. With no tracker file at all, every
check runs unconditionally — the tracker is advisory, the OVN db remains
the source of truth.

Sixteen steps:

| step | checks |
|---|---|
| `tracker` | deployment tracker state, including stale-boot entries |
| `controller` | the ovn-controller service |
| `br-int` | the integration bridge |
| `external-ids` | OVS external-ids |
| `chassis` | chassis registration in the SB db |
| `port-bindings` | the host-if port binding |
| `port-binding-sweep` | every port binding, and any missing chassis |
| `chassisredirect` | gateway port bindings |
| `gateway-pins` | gateway-chassis pins vs registered chassis |
| `isolation` | isolation port group and its scope |
| `acls` | micro-segmentation ACLs |
| `port-security` | VM port security |
| `range-overlap` | range overlap and the policy tiers |
| `flows` | OpenFlow rules on br-int |
| `host-if` | the host-facing OVS interface, and whether its configuration will survive a reboot |
| `live-arp` | live ARP/ping test of the gateway |
| `summary` | the failure/warning tally |

Result tags are `[ OK ]`, `[FAIL]`, `[WARN]`, `[SKIP]`. Exit status is 1 if
anything failed.

`check_chassisredirect` resolves the chassis **name** rather than merely
checking one is set — `Port_Binding.chassis` survives a reboot, so a
binding can name a chassis nothing is running under, and resolving the name
is the difference between a live binding and a fossil.

**`check_host_if` (step 7) reads `ofport` and `error`, not just
`iface-id`.** Every other field in the Interface row can be exactly right
while ovs-vswitchd has failed to open the netdev behind it, and in that
state `admin_state` and `link_state` render as the empty set rather than
`down` — so the row reads as healthy under an `[ OK ]` while
ovn-controller has nothing to bind and the host reaches nothing. When
`ofport` is not positive the step fails, prints what ovs-vswitchd said,
and uses `_link_kind()` to name the device kind holding the interface
name, which is usually the whole explanation.

**`check_port_binding_sweep` (step 5b) checks `ofport` before blaming the
reboot race.** ovn-controller cannot claim a port with `ofport -1`, and no
number of restarts will change that. Sending the operator to `reconcile`
there is worse than saying nothing: it looks like a fix, does nothing, and
hides the real cause behind a plausible one. The unbound-VIF branch now
distinguishes "no datapath, rebuild the port" from the genuine race, and
from the identity-drift case where a restart would actively make things
worse.

**`_check_persistence()`** runs at the end of step 7. An interface being
correct right now says nothing about whether it will be correct after a
reboot, and that is the failure people actually hit — everything green,
reboot, host-if bare again. It reports which mechanism is armed and
distinguishes the two ways the NetworkManager path silently does nothing:
a keyfile present while the device is unmanaged, and a device managed at
runtime only, with `99-ovnctl-host-if.conf` missing so the flag will be
gone at the next boot. A fully green step 7 now means the configuration
will still be there tomorrow.

Three latent faults were fixed in the port: `check_isolation` referenced an
undefined `_iso_vms`, silently breaking the live non-member proof; three
calls passed a third argument to the two-argument `cfg()`, which exits on a
miss; and `require_cfg` demanded `[localnet_external].br_asa` / `.phys_nic`,
which no longer exist, so the whole script aborted before running a single
check on a single-NIC host.

### `reconcile.py` (700 lines) — `ovnctl reconcile`

**No shell ancestor.** `deploy` builds a deployment and `delete` removes
one; neither answers the question a reboot asks.

What survives a reboot:

- `conf.db` — bridges, ports, external-ids, iface-ids
- the NB db — switches, routers, ACLs, gateway pins
- the SB db — `Port_Binding` rows, *including their chassis column*, which
  is why a stale binding reads as "up" long after it stopped meaning
  anything

What does not:

- `external-ids:system-id`, which `ovs-ctl start --system-id=random`
  rewrites from `/etc/openvswitch/system-id.conf` at every boot
- the kernel side of `host-if`: link state, address, routes
- any claim ovn-controller had on a VIF whose tap is recreated after it

Steps: `external-ids`, `identity`, `gateway-pins`, `host-interface`,
`claim-check`, `acl-logging`, `tracker`.

Everything is idempotent; running it when nothing is wrong changes nothing.
It reuses `setup.Setup` for the host-interface work rather than
reimplementing it.

`--repair-identity` additionally deletes stale chassis rows.

#### `--install-unit`

Writes `/etc/systemd/system/ovn-reconcile.service` and enables it.
Ordered after `ovn-controller.service`, the Open vSwitch unit and
`NetworkManager.service`: reconcile compares the registered chassis
against the configured identity and there is nothing to compare against
until the controller has connected to the SB db, and NetworkManager goes
first so that reconcile only has to fix what NM did not.

All the dependencies are `Wants=`, never `Requires=`. The unit name for
Open vSwitch differs between distributions (`openvswitch.service`,
`ovs-vswitchd.service`, `openvswitch-switch.service`), and a `Requires=`
on one that does not exist here fails the job outright — silently, because
a cancelled job is not a failed one and nothing appears in
`systemctl status ovn-reconcile`. The single thing that repairs a boot
must not be able to disappear that quietly.

#### `--install-nm-profile`

Covers the host-if half declaratively instead of once per boot. It
generates `/etc/NetworkManager/system-connections/host-if.nmconnection`
from `[setup]` in `ovn-settings.yaml` — `host_if_ip`/`host_if_prefix` as
the address and every `host_routes` entry as a static route via
`lrp_host_cidr`, each at `host_route_metric`. The config stays the single
source of truth, so the profile cannot drift from what `setup` and
`reconcile` apply. Preview with `-n`.

Three things about the generated profile are load-bearing, and each was a
failure that presented as "correct now, bare after a reboot":

**The profile type is read from the device, not assumed.**
`_nm_device_type()` asks NetworkManager what it thinks `host-if` is
before the file is generated. An OVS internal port has driver
`openvswitch`, which NM does not recognise as ethernet, so it types the
device `openvswitch` or `generic` depending on version — and NM refuses to
activate a connection whose type does not match the device's. An
`802-3-ethernet` profile there does not half-work; it writes cleanly,
`nmcli connection reload` accepts it, and it then silently never
activates. A generic connection has no `cloned-mac-address` property,
which costs nothing: the MAC lives in the OVS Interface row, where it
belongs and where it survives a reboot on its own. If NM reports
`ovs-interface` — its OVS plugin is loaded and reading ovsdb — the command
refuses, because the only profile NM would accept then needs an
`ovs-port` and `ovs-bridge` profile above it, handing br-int to
NetworkManager.

**The managed flag has to be persistent.** NetworkManager leaves OVS
internal ports unmanaged on many hosts, and an unmanaged device never
autoconnects, so the keyfile sits on disk doing nothing.
`nmcli device set host-if managed yes` writes to `/run/NetworkManager` and
so lasts exactly until the next reboot — the one moment it needs to hold.
The command therefore also writes
`/etc/NetworkManager/conf.d/99-ovnctl-host-if.conf` with a
`[device-ovnctl-host-if]` section setting `managed=1`, the persistent form
of the same flag, which outranks `[keyfile].unmanaged-devices`. That file
is written and applied (`systemctl reload NetworkManager` — `nmcli
connection reload` only rescans keyfiles) *before* the device type is
read, because NM's answer to "what type is this device" is not dependable
while the device is unmanaged.

**No `gateway=`, and `never-default=true`.** An `ipv4.gateway` on host-if
installs a default route through `lr-core` that then carries the host's
entire egress. The host reaches the VM segments through the explicit
`host_routes` only.

`_nm_conflicting_profiles()` refuses to write at all while another NM
profile of a device-creating type (`NM_DEVICE_CREATING_TYPES` — `dummy`,
`bridge`, `bond`, `ovs-interface`, …) is bound to the same interface name,
and names it. Such a profile wins the race for the device name at every
boot and leaves the OVS port with no datapath; see `setup._reclaim_name()`.

A failed activation prints nmcli's own error text rather than telling the
operator to re-run the command to see it, and a successful one re-reads
`GENERAL.STATE`: `unmanaged` means something outranks the conf.d snippet,
and `connected (externally)` means NM is *describing* configuration
someone else applied rather than applying this profile — it reads as
connected while the profile is not in force, so the device looks fine and
still comes back bare.

#### Files written on the host

| path | written by | purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `/etc/systemd/system/ovn-reconcile.service` | `--install-unit` | run `reconcile` at every boot |
| `/etc/NetworkManager/system-connections/host-if.nmconnection` | `--install-nm-profile` | address + routes, reapplied whenever the device appears |
| `/etc/NetworkManager/conf.d/99-ovnctl-host-if.conf` | `--install-nm-profile` | keep host-if managed across reboots |

Both mechanisms are worth having. The keyfile reapplies whenever the
device appears, including after an ovs-vswitchd restart that recreates the
internal port mid-boot; the unit covers what NetworkManager cannot see —
`system-id`, gateway pins, and rebuilding the OVS port itself if it comes
back with no datapath.

### `delete.py` (645 lines) — `ovnctl delete` (alias `teardown`)

Port of `ovndelete.sh`. Reverses the OVN/OVS config so you can start from
scratch.

Everything is **discovered dynamically** rather than matched against
hardcoded names, so it works regardless of what names were used to create
the switches, routers, ports and bridges. Only `br-int` gets special
handling, since it is OVN's own integration bridge rather than an uplink
bridge to remove outright.

Steps:

1. `host-networking` — release NetworkManager, then flush addresses/routes
   on br-int internal ports
2. `ovs-ports` — remove those OVS ports
3. `uplink-bridges` — remove every other OVS bridge and free its NIC (and
   re-manage that NIC in NetworkManager)
4. `logical-config` — remove all logical routers and switches
5. `port-groups` — remove all port groups and their ACLs
6. `lb-meters` — remove load balancers and meters
7. `stop-pcap` — stop captures and remove mirrors
8. `stop-controller` — stop ovn-controller
9. `chassis` — remove `Chassis` / `Chassis_Private` rows
10. `external-ids` — clear the OVS external-ids
11. `purge-db` — *(`--purge-db` only)* wipe the raw NB/SB database files
12. `br-int` — remove br-int and verify it stays gone
13. `verify-clean` — confirm the SB db is empty
14. `tracker` — erase the deployment tracker
15. `show` — *(`-v`)* dump `ovs-vsctl` / `ovn-nbctl show`

**`_nm_release()`** runs before the flush in step 1. Once
`reconcile --install-nm-profile` has run, NetworkManager owns the address
and routes on host-if: it sees them disappear and puts them straight back,
so the teardown reports the interface flushed while the host still carries
`172.31.x` and routes into a topology that no longer exists. That is worse
than not flushing at all, because it looks finished. The profile itself is
deliberately left on disk — it is generated from `ovn-settings.yaml`,
costs nothing while the device is gone, and NM reapplies it as soon as
`deploy` recreates the port. Deleting it would mean every delete/deploy
cycle silently reverted the host to not-reboot-safe until someone
remembered to re-run `--install-nm-profile`.

`--purge-db` is belt-and-braces on top of the object-level wipe, which
already removes everything logically. `--keep-br-int` leaves br-int in
place. `stop_controller_hard()` exists because a controller that
re-registers as fast as rows are deleted defeats the chassis step.

### `deploy.py` (200 lines) — `ovnctl deploy`

Port of `ovn-deploy.sh`. Runs the deployment in order.

Each stage **is** the corresponding subcommand, re-entered in-process via
`cli.run_argv` with the same global flags, so `ovnctl deploy --from acl`
and `ovnctl acl` do exactly the same work.

| stage | kind |
|---|---|
| `setup` | required |
| `localnet-internal` | required |
| `localnet-external` | required |
| `vm-config` | required |
| `vm-isolation` | required |
| `acl` | required |
| `vm-attach` | optional |
| `pcap --start` | optional |

Required stages abort the run if they fail. Optional stages report and
continue — they depend on things outside OVN (libvirt, tcpdump, running
VMs) and are expected to no-op on some hosts, so a missing host-side tool
must not silently swallow everything after it.

Because it uses the same step framework, `--from vm-config` resumes a
half-finished build and `--skip pcap` drops a stage. `--verify` runs
`diagnose` once the deployment finishes.

### `runbook.py` (244 lines) — `ovnctl runbook`

Port of `ovn-runbook.sh`. Emits every command the automation would run as a
single clean, copy-pasteable shell script.

Distinct from both `--dry-run` on one subcommand (a single component) and
`ovnctl -n deploy` (chained, with banners): this produces a self-contained
runbook you can hand to someone without the tooling, paste into a change
record, or diff between two configurations.

It works by capturing each subcommand's dry-run output in-process
(`contextlib.redirect_stdout` around `cli.run_argv`) and wrapping it in
section headers with a generated preamble (hostname, chassis id,
timestamp).

Flags: `-o FILE`, `--teardown`, `--all` (deployment then teardown),
`--no-header` (commands only, no comments).

**Important caveat, stated in the module docstring and the README:** the
output reflects the *current* state of the system, because every subcommand
queries before deciding what to change. On a fully deployed host you get
only the delta. For the full build sequence, generate it from a clean host
or after a teardown.

### `show.py` (832 lines) — `ovnctl show`

Port of the standalone `tools/ovn-show`. Cisco-style read-only views.

| view | shows |
|---|---|
| `chassis` | SB registration, gateway pins, `cr-` bindings |
| `routers` | logical routers and their ports |
| `switches` | logical switches and their ports |
| `ip-int-br` | router ports and addresses |
| `interfaces` | switch ports and attached VIFs |
| `route` | the logical routing table |
| `policies` | logical router policies (reroute rules) |
| `acls` | switch-level **and** port-group ACLs |

No argument prints everything. `_ALIASES` accepts the singular/plural and
`show-`-prefixed spellings.

Nothing here mutates anything, so it does not honour `--dry-run`. The
original shipped with `shell=True` string commands; this runs everything as
argv lists instead, for the same output.

The bulk of the file is OVSDB JSON normalisation (`extract_ovsdb_ports`,
`parse_ovsdb_map`, `parse_ovsdb_bool`, `extract_ovsdb_set`,
`extract_ovsdb_scalar`, `_uuid_of`) plus a set of `get_*_map()` correlators.

Three details:

- `get_port_binding_map()` returns `None` — not `{}` — if the SB db could
  not be queried at all, so callers can distinguish "SB unreachable" from
  "port genuinely unbound". Conflating those turns every port into a false
  DOWN when the tool is run without root.
- `get_acl_details_map()` tries the query *with* the `name` column first
  and falls back to one without it, since `name` is a newer addition and
  older deployments would otherwise return nothing at all.
- `_vm_domain()` resolves a port to a VM by consulting virsh first (the
  actual running domain) and falling back to the `external-ids:vm-name` tag
  `vm-config` sets — which still resolves when the libvirt domain doesn't
  exist or isn't running. All three views use the same correlator; the
  port-group view previously read the tag only, so the same port appeared
  as two different machines depending on which section you looked at.

`show_chassis()` additionally warns when multiple chassis are registered on
a single-node deployment (likely system-id drift) or when the local
`system-id` matches no registered chassis at all.

