## 6. Behavioural fixes carried in the port

The shell suite had drifted out of sync with its own configuration and did
not run on a clean checkout. These differences are deliberate.

**`localnet-external` and `diagnose` aborted immediately.** Both called
`require_cfg` for a `[localnet_external]` block and an `[asa_addressing]`
section that no longer exist. Policy-only is now the primary path; the
legacy physical-uplink steps run only when those keys are actually present,
so both topologies work.

**`diagnose` had three latent faults** — an undefined `_iso_vms` breaking
the live isolation proof, three `cfg` calls passing a default to a
two-argument function that exits on a miss, and the abort above meaning a
single-NIC host could not be diagnosed at all.

**`next_hop` validation was documented but never implemented.** Now an
error when set explicitly and off-link, a warning when inherited from
`lan_gateway`.

**`--dry-run` wrote to the deployment tracker.** Previewing `ovn-setup.sh`
left a `setup` flag behind and every later diagnose believed the deployment
existed. Tracker writes are now no-ops in a dry run.

**A partial run no longer claims a full deployment.** Recording `setup`
after `--only switches` made diagnose expect a router, gateway ports and a
host interface that were never created.

**`ovn-internal-ranges.conf` is parsed, not sourced.** The shell `source`d
it, so a stray command in a config file would execute.

**`unit_exists` no longer uses `systemctl … | grep -q`.** grep exits on
first match, systemctl takes SIGPIPE, and the pipeline reports failure
despite having matched.

### Fixes made after the port, chasing one bug

The following all came out of a single investigation into "host-if has no
address after a reboot", and are listed together because each one masked
the next.

**A broken OVS port was never rebuilt.** `host-interface` skipped
`add-port` whenever a port row existed, conflating the row with a working
interface. A row with `ofport -1` therefore survived every `setup` and
every `reconcile` indefinitely, both of which went on re-asserting the
`iface-id` on it.

**`diagnose` reported that broken port as healthy.** Step 7 checked
`iface-id` and printed `admin_state`/`link_state` as an informational
detail, returning `[ OK ]` regardless — and an unopened interface renders
those as the empty set, not `down`.

**`diagnose` then misattributed the resulting unbound port to the reboot
race** and recommended `reconcile`, which cannot fix a port with no
`ofport`. A recommendation that looks like a fix and does nothing is worse
than none.

**A device-creating NetworkManager profile could take the interface name
at every boot**, leaving the OVS port with no datapath. `setup` now
reclaims the name from a `dummy` or `veth` placeholder;
`--install-nm-profile` refuses to write while such a profile exists.

**The NetworkManager keyfile was `type=ethernet`.** NM types an OVS
internal port `openvswitch` or `generic` and refuses to activate a
connection whose type does not match the device's, so the profile wrote
cleanly and then never activated once. The type is now read from the
device before the file is generated.

**The managed flag was set at runtime only.** `nmcli device set … managed
yes` writes to `/run`, so an OVS internal port that NM leaves unmanaged by
default came back unmanaged at every boot and the keyfile never
autoconnected. Now persisted in `conf.d`.

**Host routes were installed twice.** NetworkManager and `ip route
replace` used different metrics, and `replace` matches on *(destination,
metric)*, so neither owner could see the other's copy. Both now use
`[setup].host_route_metric`.

**`--install-unit` could be cancelled silently.** `Requires=` on an Open
vSwitch unit name that does not exist on this distribution fails the job,
and a cancelled job leaves nothing in `systemctl status`.

**`delete` reported host networking flushed while NetworkManager put it
straight back.** Step 1 now deactivates the profile first.

**A disabled `pcap` failed the stage.** `--start` with
`[pcap].enabled: false` now exits 0 instead of aborting, so a host that
has chosen not to capture stops printing an error and a skipped stage on
every deploy.

Two behaviours preserved on purpose, both verified by `diagnose`:

- Port-group ACL matches are always scoped with `inport`/`outport ==
  @<group>`. An unscoped match applies switch-wide — this caused a real
  outage once.
- `ovn-trace` verdicts match `ct_mark.blocked = 1` as well as `drop;`.
  Once any ACL is `allow-related` the pipeline is stateful and northd
  compiles a drop into a `ct_commit` with no `output`, so matching only
  `drop;` reports stateful drops as ALLOW.